{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts sit ~5–15 EUR/t above realized prices across the whole 30‑day horizon; apply a downward level shift (larger when recent history is at local highs).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: after an uptrend peak, the model continues projecting elevated/sideways prices while the market mean-reverts lower; introduce a regime switch that increases negative drift when the last 5–10 days show decelerating gains or a rollover.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: when the last observed prices are near recent maxima, the model extrapolates the spike; cap upside continuation and force partial reversion toward a short MA (e.g., 10–20d) over the next 1–2 weeks.",
    "Horizon-specific error grows with time: overprediction is modest in days 1–5 but widens materially beyond ~day 7–10; apply an increasing downward adjustment with forecast horizon (e.g., linear ramp).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast path is too smooth and too high versus realized drawdowns; increase downside responsiveness (higher beta to negative returns) and reduce persistence of positive shocks."
  ]
}
